  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on a mechanical calculation of the arithmetic mean of outcomes, ignoring probabilities. The individual did consider the outcomes of both lotteries, but did not perform any such calculation. Instead, they emphasized the 'fair chance' of getting a high outcome in B and dismissed the 0 outcome due to its low probability—this shows a sensitivity to probability, which the decision reason explicitly ignores. Moreover, the reason would favor A, but the individual’s verbal leaning (though not explicitly stating a choice) seems to give positive weight to B having two high outcomes. There is no evidence the individual summed outcomes or divided by the number of outcomes. The information considered (presence of high outcomes, perceived fairness of chance, neglect of low-probability event) does not align with the reason’s rule (mean of outcomes regardless of probability).",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
}

